From: pedro <pedrocarvs26-ops@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 16:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] init: let the vendor init library replace the weak stub

TARGET_INIT_VENDOR_LIB does not work.  libinit compiles the weak
vendor_load_properties() stub from vendor_init.cpp into itself and pulls
the vendor library in with whole_static_libs, so libinit.a ends up
holding two definitions of the symbol.  Soong archives the module's own
objects first and appends the whole-static-libs members afterwards:

  rm -f $out && $arCmd $arObjFlags $out $arObjs \
             && $arCmd $arLibFlags $out $arLibs

so vendor_init.o precedes the vendor library's object in the archive.
When the linker resolves the reference from property_service.cpp it
fetches the first archive member that defines the symbol, and a weak
definition is still a definition, so the stub wins and the vendor code
is never pulled in at all.  It is silent: the build succeeds, init
runs, and none of the vendor properties are ever set.

  $ ar crsD libinit.a vendor_init.o property_service.o init_sec.o
  $ clang++ main.o libinit.a -o t && ./t
  STUB
  $ ar crsD libinit.a init_sec.o vendor_init.o property_service.o
  $ clang++ main.o libinit.a -o t && ./t
  INIT_SEC

Drop the stub from libinit when a vendor library is configured, which
leaves exactly one definition and makes the override deterministic
rather than dependent on archive order.
---
 init/Android.bp | 1 +
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+)

diff --git a/init/Android.bp b/init/Android.bp
--- a/init/Android.bp
+++ b/init/Android.bp
@@ -156,6 +156,7 @@
     product_variables: {
         lineage: {
             target_init_vendor_lib: {
+                exclude_srcs: ["vendor_init.cpp"],
                 whole_static_libs: ["%s"],
             },
         },
